WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:06.960
So we're going to talk about the origin, the mathematical origins of Tetris. The quite well-known computer game.

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:10.809
And

00:00:10.809 --> 00:00:12.040
I

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:15.359
thought about this because recently I was on a holiday in Crete,

00:00:15.429 --> 00:00:18.719
and I was walking down the side, with all these people selling, kind of, tourist rubbish.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:24.810
But there was this guy who was selling these really interesting, like, math and logic puzzles - including this one here

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:29.879
which I bought. So this is a puzzle where it is full of these elements here,

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:36.239
which- these are not quite Tetris because Tetris is four squares, these have 5. So these are 5 squares.

00:00:36.239 --> 00:00:41.249
So these are all the possible ways you can put 5 squares together, apart from this, and

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:46.020
rather brilliantly- so there are 12: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6,

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:49.259
7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12. There are 12 ways

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:53.849
you can put 5 squares together, and these are all the possible ones. And it turns out

00:00:54.129 --> 00:00:57.149
that you can put them all together, and there's several ways to do it

00:00:57.149 --> 00:01:02.069
but they're quite difficult to find, you can put them in at the square and all you need extra is the 4x4.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:04.119
So this is the

00:01:04.119 --> 00:01:05.439
the sort of the runt of the family.

00:01:05.439 --> 00:01:10.589
Well, yeah, you can do it because you know that there are 12 and there are five of these, and so 5 times

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:13.260
12 is what?

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:18.839
(Brady: 60.)
- Yes, 60 and 4 is 64, which is 8x8.

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:20.220
So obviously you know

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:24.449
the numbers are going to work, and it's just finding it and can it work?

00:01:24.729 --> 00:01:30.868
And the reason why I wanted to buy this is because there is a person who is the first person to discover

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:38.160
that you can get all these things with 5 squares in, into a biggest 8x8 square: and his name was

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:41.140
Henry Dudeney, or Henry Ernest Dudeney,

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:46.240
known to his friends and friends as Ernest; who was Britain's greatest inventor of puzzles.

00:01:46.259 --> 00:01:50.639
I would say the greatest inventor of mathematical puzzles the world has ever seen

00:01:51.189 --> 00:01:52.450
because

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:54.600
the depth and the variety of the

00:01:54.850 --> 00:02:02.369
mathematical puzzles that he did beats anyone else. And lots of Americans would say no it was Sam Loyd. Sam Loyd, they were contemporaries,

00:02:02.369 --> 00:02:05.758
they actually hated each other. Well, they liked each other then they fell out so they hated each other.

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:09.300
Whereas Sam Loyd probably did more puzzles, was a bit more of

00:02:10.209 --> 00:02:14.369
salesman, and he also did some amazing puzzles, but you don't find many mathematicians

00:02:15.069 --> 00:02:18.359
studying Sam Loyd puzzles. But you find

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.029
still there are lots Dudeney puzzles that

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:27.750
people are interested in, including this one here. And this one first appeared in 1907 in a book called

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:34.500
The Canterbury puzzles, which all kind of had historical stories to them. And the story was, so William the Conqueror,

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:40.709
he's the person who came away from France and like invaded England, and then became King of England and he had two sons. And his sons

00:02:41.319 --> 00:02:43.319
went to France and

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:46.979
stayed with the son of the French King at the time,

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:49.620
it's kind of just, sort of,

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:56.910
slightly apocryphal history. And apparently they got into a fight, and the fight was over a game of chess, because the British were beating the French.

00:02:57.250 --> 00:02:58.900
Blows were

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.799
given. Basically they, yeah started fighting and

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:04.949
the chessboard was whacked over the head of someone and it

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:11.669
collapsed into thirteen different pieces. And the thirteen different pieces that are drawn in the 1907

00:03:12.069 --> 00:03:19.858
Canterbury puzzles are exactly these pieces. And this is the first time in the mathematical literature that you have the selection of

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.060
all the possible

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:26.220
shapes that you can get when you put five, or in fact any number, of

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:28.150
squares

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:33.390
orthogonally next to each other. So I- they've got to be either sort of straight on, Dudeney

00:03:33.489 --> 00:03:35.909
also did another puzzle ten years later

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:43.139
which was all about stamps and folding stamps. And there he said, what are all the different ways you can tear out four stamps together?

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:50.639
And that was the first time that there were ever the appearance of Tetris blocks. Because if you have four squares together,

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:54.449
that's what Tetris is. And so Dudeney, the first time ever

00:03:54.450 --> 00:04:00.450
he did any type of block were the five ones in '07, and then in '17 there were the four ones. Now in the

00:04:00.819 --> 00:04:03.809
1960s this American mathematician called Solomon Golomb

00:04:04.239 --> 00:04:09.509
started looking at that, and started to classify them and do lots of maths on them. And he starts to call them

00:04:10.569 --> 00:04:12.659
poly-ominoes. So the five one are

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:17.160
pentominoes, and this is the set of the fi- of 12 pentominoes.

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:21.720
There are tetrominoes, which are the ones with four. And there are the triominoes,

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:26.329
and then there's the Dominoes which are the one with the two. And it was because of

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:30.170
Solomon Golomb's work, in the 1980s a

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:32.550
Soviet

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:37.850
computer guy was reading Golomb's work - which, Golomb's work references Dudeney's work; and

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:41.869
basically invented Tetris, which is the tetrominoes

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:49.309
falling and you have to stack them. And, I dunno, this is basically - if it was called "Petris" this is basically what it would be like.

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:57.529
I got this because of the hor- the historical-ness of it, I thought isn't it interesting that this is the very puzzle that begat

00:04:57.870 --> 00:05:05.840
Tetris. And Here I am, in Greece, and a guy's selling it. And so I bought it, and it is incredibly satisfying to do.

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:07.110
(Brady: Come on, let's see you do it.)

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:11.480
So, it would probably - takes me each time. 'Cause you always forget how you stack them.

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:17.029
And each time you do it, it probably takes me about 15-20 minutes? And you think until you've done it

00:05:17.030 --> 00:05:20.510
there's no way I'm gonna be able to do it. And then when you get it, you just do get it.

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:23.810
And you sort of work out little techniques, you know,

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:26.780
these ones here like much more complicated so it's quite good to put that there.

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:31.669
And also there's another one, this one here gets in the way so I can often start with something like this.

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:33.860
Maybe let's put this in the corner.

00:05:34.919 --> 00:05:42.649
It's so sort of tactile and it's lovely. And when you get it it's really, really, really satisfying.

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:47.768
We've made a second video about another famous Dudeney puzzle.

00:05:47.768 --> 00:05:50.179
[Preview] What I want, I want you to get them there to

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:54.200
here in three moves.

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:54.860
Also

